  <h1>Editorial Team</h1>
  <p>elderabusecalc.com is written and reviewed by a small editorial team at The Click Lab. Elder abuse and nursing home litigation is unusually demanding content territory: the legal framework spans state Elder Abuse Acts, federal nursing home regulations (OBRA 1987 and the CMS F-Tag survey system), state Adult Protective Services statutes, and ordinary tort law. The medical content involves pressure ulcer staging, geriatric pharmacology, falls and fracture management, and the natural history of malnutrition and sepsis in elderly patients. Getting the content right means working from primary sources: the state Elder Abuse Act and APS statutes, the OBRA regulations and CMS State Operations Manual Appendix PP (where the F-Tags live), the leading appellate decisions on heightened damages, peer-reviewed geriatric medical literature, and the published verdict and settlement databases that track elder abuse outcomes.</p>

  <h2>How we work</h2>
  <p>Every guide follows the same workflow. A contributor drafts the article working from primary sources &mdash; the controlling state Elder Abuse Act, the OBRA regulations and CMS F-Tag definitions, ombudsman program documentation, state Attorney General elder abuse reports, peer-reviewed geriatric medicine literature, and the published verdict databases that track nursing home and elder abuse outcomes. The editor-in-chief reviews the draft for accuracy of citation, internal consistency of damages analysis, and whether it actually answers a question a real reader (often a family member of a nursing home resident, sometimes the resident themselves, occasionally an attorney just entering the practice area) would ask. The calculator team verifies any formulas or worked examples against published settlement distributions. The final piece is published with the reviewer&rsquo;s byline.</p>
  <p>We don&rsquo;t publish thin content. Every guide is intended to answer a specific question a non-lawyer reader might ask &mdash; how do I report suspected abuse, what is a Stage IV pressure ulcer, why does the F-Tag system matter for litigation, what is the typical recovery in a wrongful death case from neglect, what does California &sect; 15657 actually do &mdash; with enough specificity to be useful while being honest about the limits of general informational content. Many readers are reaching the site in an emotionally difficult moment; we aim for clarity, sensitivity, and accuracy.</p>

  <h2>Contributors</h2>

  <h3>Marlowe Quinn (MQ) &mdash; Editor-in-Chief &amp; Elder Abuse Practice Lead</h3>
  <p><em>Paralegal &mdash; elder law and nursing home litigation.</em></p>
  <p>Marlowe has spent the last 12 years working at firms specializing in elder abuse, nursing home neglect, and elder financial exploitation. They have hands-on exposure to thousands of pages of facility records, CMS survey histories, APS investigative files, and the specialized expert work (wound-care experts, geriatricians, life-care planners) that elder abuse cases require. Marlowe wrote the bulk of the compensation methodology content on this site and reviews every piece for technical accuracy against the underlying statutory, regulatory, and medical sources. They cite the state Elder Abuse Act, the OBRA regulations, and the F-Tag definitions the way other writers cite sources of inspiration. They are not a licensed attorney; their role on this site is editorial.</p>

  <h3>Reed Stafford (RS2) &mdash; State APS &amp; Long-Term Care Reference</h3>
  <p><em>Paralegal &mdash; long-term care, Medicaid, and Adult Protective Services intake.</em></p>
  <p>Reed focuses on the public-system side of elder abuse: state Adult Protective Services intake and investigation procedures, the long-term care ombudsman programs, the Medicaid framework that finances most U.S. nursing home care, and the federal-state regulatory architecture that produces the F-Tag survey results. Many readers are family members trying to understand whether to call APS, what an ombudsman can and can&rsquo;t do, and how to interpret a CMS deficiency report; Reed writes the content explaining these systems in plain language. They have a granular sense of how facility deficiencies actually translate into litigation evidence. They are not a licensed attorney or social worker; their role is to make complex regulatory and protective-services concepts understandable.</p>

  <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cite the authority.</strong> Every rule we discuss is grounded in a specific source &mdash; a state Elder Abuse Act, an OBRA regulation, a CMS F-Tag definition, an APS statute, an appellate opinion, or peer-reviewed geriatric medical literature. When we describe a rule, we name the source.</li>
    <li><strong>No legal or medical advice.</strong> Everything on this site is general and informational. Elder abuse cases require evaluation by an experienced elder abuse attorney and ongoing medical care by qualified geriatric providers. We don&rsquo;t review individual cases or substitute for professional consultation.</li>
    <li><strong>No paid placements.</strong> We don&rsquo;t accept money, gifts, or other consideration to recommend law firms, attorneys, nursing facilities, or care providers. The only ads on the site are programmatic Google AdSense ads; we don&rsquo;t choose what runs.</li>
    <li><strong>Plain language first.</strong> Elder abuse terms (<em>F-Tag</em>, <em>EADACPA</em>, <em>APS</em>, <em>ombudsman</em>, <em>survey deficiency</em>, <em>statutory enhancement</em>, <em>treble damages</em>, <em>survival statute</em>, <em>wrongful death overlay</em>) are defined where they&rsquo;re first used. We assume the reader is intelligent but not formally trained.</li>
    <li><strong>Worked examples.</strong> Abstract numbers without context are hard to apply. Most guides include at least one worked example with realistic facts and recovery analysis.</li>
    <li><strong>Sensitive to context.</strong> Many readers arrive at this site during a difficult moment &mdash; sometimes during an active abuse situation, sometimes after a parent&rsquo;s death. We aim for content that&rsquo;s substantive without being clinical, informative without being alarmist, and that consistently directs readers to APS, the ombudsman, and qualified counsel for actual decisions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sources we rely on</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>State Elder Abuse Acts and APS statutes</strong> &mdash; particularly California Welfare &amp; Institutions Code &sect; 15600 et seq. (EADACPA), Florida Chapter 415, Texas Human Resources Code Chapter 48, and the Illinois Nursing Home Care Act.</li>
    <li><strong>OBRA 1987 and the federal nursing home regulations</strong> at 42 CFR Part 483.</li>
    <li><strong>CMS State Operations Manual, Appendix PP</strong> &mdash; the source document for the F-Tag system.</li>
    <li><strong>State Long-Term Care Ombudsman program reports.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>National Center on Elder Abuse</strong> &mdash; Eldercare Locator hotline (1-800-677-1116), prevalence research, and clearinghouse for state APS information.</li>
    <li><strong>Peer-reviewed geriatric medical literature</strong> &mdash; particularly on pressure ulcer staging (NPUAP / NPIAP), falls and hip fracture outcomes, geriatric pharmacology, and the natural history of neglect-related decline.</li>
    <li><strong>Verdict and settlement databases</strong> &mdash; the published elder abuse and nursing home databases maintained by VerdictSearch, Jury Verdict Research, and the specialty publications that track elder abuse outcomes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If you find an error &mdash; an outdated F-Tag number, a calculator output that doesn&rsquo;t match the underlying formula, an incorrect statutory citation, a misstated APS reporting threshold &mdash; please email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a> with the page URL and what&rsquo;s wrong. Verified corrections are typically made within a few business days. Material corrections are noted at the bottom of the affected page.</p>

  <h2>Reviewer disclosures</h2>
  <p>Our contributors are not licensed attorneys, medical providers, or social workers. They are credentialed paralegals with substantive experience in elder abuse practice and long-term care regulation. Reviewing legal and medical content for accuracy is part of these professionals&rsquo; skill sets and is not the same as giving legal, medical, or social-work advice. Nothing on this site should be construed as legal or medical advice, and nothing here creates an attorney-client or treating-provider relationship.</p>

  <h2>Contact the editorial team</h2>
  <p>Editorial questions, citation suggestions, or interview requests: <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We respond within a few business days. If you believe abuse is happening right now, please contact Adult Protective Services or the Eldercare Locator hotline (1-800-677-1116) first &mdash; editorial inquiries are not an emergency channel.</p>
